gsipper (1.3.36-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Bring back the no-'is ready'-notification panel-icon click
    that 1.3.34 broke. Two changes versus 1.3.34/1.3.35:
      - Click handling moved off vfunc_event back onto the
        button-press-event signal. vfunc_event runs before
        gnome-shell has finished registering the click, so the
        Activate that fires from there has no xdg-activation
        token attached, and the compositor falls back to the
        'gsipper is ready' notification. button-press-event
        runs late enough for the activation context to exist.
      - Activate now goes through Shell.App.activate() rather
        than a raw Gio.DBus.session.call('Activate', {}).
        Shell.App.activate() is gnome-shell's own canonical
        path: it pulls the activation token from the latest
        input event and bakes it into the Activate D-Bus call's
        platform_data automatically — same code path the
        app-grid uses, same focus-stealing-bypass behaviour.
    Right-click popup now lives on `this._statusMenu` (not
    `this.menu`), so PanelMenu.Button's auto-toggle on every
    click stays disabled — only our own button-press-event
    handler can open it, and only on button 3.
    extension/metadata.json version 9 -> 10.

 -- pulpoff <noreply@pulpoff.com>  Tue, 20 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000

gsipper (1.3.35-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Registration retry backoff: after three consecutive
    REGISTER failures, gsipper now pauses auto-retry for two
    minutes before resuming. PJSIP's own retry interval is
    tightened from 300 s to 30 s (regConfig.retryIntervalSec /
    firstRetryIntervalSec) so the three attempts within each
    'group' feel responsive — three failures land in ~90 s,
    then a 120 s breather, then three more, and so on. Any
    success during the cycle resets the failure counter +
    cancels the pause; clicking Connect / Disconnect /
    Reconnect on the status dot does too.
  * Window menu: drop the trailing '…' from Account / Settings
    / Log entries. The ellipsis is supposed to mean 'opens a
    dialog you can cancel' and Adwaita / GTK4 itself doesn't
    use it for the equivalent menu items any more; without it
    the menu reads less noisy.

