gsipper (1.3.19-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Incoming calls were silently auto-declining with 486 Busy in
    favourites-only / start-minimized / hide-to-tray setups. The
    gsipper log showed our own safe_hangup(status_code=486) firing
    while pjsua2 was still in EARLY — i.e. before the call could
    be answered — so the source had to be RinginWindow's
    close-request signal handler emitting 'decline-requested'
    without any user click. Root cause: RinginWindow was created
    with transient_for=MainWindow. When MainWindow is hidden,
    Mutter / Wayland immediately destroys the transient because
    its parent isn't mapped; the destruction emits close-request
    on the way out, and our handler turns that into a decline.
    Fix: drop set_transient_for entirely. The ringin popup now
    stands on its own (registered with the app via
    set_application so the WM groups it correctly), so it stays
    alive regardless of MainWindow visibility.
  * Belt-and-suspenders: MainWindow._open_ringin now calls
    self.set_visible(True) if the window is hidden, so even
    without the transient_for tear-down the user has somewhere
    for the WM to focus.
  * Marshal the 'incoming' state event via GLib.idle_add instead
    of calling _on_call_state_internal directly from the pjsua2
    callback thread. The previous direct call ran RinginWindow
    construction + Gio.Notification dispatch on the wrong thread,
    which on Wayland was a likely contributor to the silent
    teardown.

 -- pulpoff <noreply@pulpoff.com>  Tue, 20 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000

gsipper (1.3.18-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Incoming calls were being auto-rejected with 486 Busy and the
    ringin popup never appeared. From the user's Asterisk log:
      gsipper sends 180 Ringing -> immediately also sends 486 Busy.
    Root cause: _active_call wasn't being cleared between calls in
    every code path (likely after an early-state outgoing whose
    'ended' callback was racing with the next incoming on the
    pjsua2 callback thread). _on_incoming_call_internal saw the
    stale ref and took the 'second concurrent call' branch.
    Fix: before sending 486 we now check the previous call's state
    — if it's STATE_ENDED, we clear _active_call as stale and
    accept the new incoming. Genuinely-active second calls still
    get 486 as before.
  * Microphone indicator (top-bar) stayed lit after a call ended:
    PJSUA keeps the OS capture device open for the lifetime of
    the endpoint by default. After every call's 'ended' state we
    now submit ep.audDevManager().setNullDev() on the SIP worker
    thread to route audio to a null sink and release the mic
    handle. The next call's onCallMediaState calls setSndDev(-1,
    -1) before bridging mic <-> remote audio to restore the
    user's system defaults.

